WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.000
Welcome to Module 6 of the Privileged Access
learning path: PIM for Azure resources.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.459
This module serves the project plan's
task A3.2 in Phase 3, Azure privileged

3
00:00:11.459 --> 00:00:16.650
roles eligible through PIM, with
Chapter 06 of the developer workbook.

4
00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.807
Owner, Contributor and
User Access Administrator

5
00:00:19.807 --> 00:00:22.137
on a subscription are privileged too.

6
00:00:22.137 --> 00:00:27.075
The plan makes them eligible through PIM,
with at most three subscription owners.

7
00:00:27.075 --> 00:00:30.513
In this module you hold PIM
for Azure resources as code.

8
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:34.983
One reviewed file says which scopes are
governed, the settings for each tier,

9
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:39.737
who is eligible, and which standing
assignments are allowed and who owns each one.

10
00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:42.179
The code reads the estate, prints a diff,

11
00:00:42.179 --> 00:00:46.224
writes the requests it would send,
and sends them only with Apply.

12
00:00:46.224 --> 00:00:50.440
It's illustrative: tested
against fixtures and a fake.

13
00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.390
Why its own module?

14
00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:59.215
Because PIM for Azure resources
isn't in Microsoft Graph.

15
00:00:59.215 --> 00:01:01.433
It goes through Azure Resource Manager,

16
00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:05.803
and the caller needs Azure roles on the
resource instead of Graph permissions.

17
00:01:05.803 --> 00:01:11.690
The roles matter: Owner, Contributor and
User Access Administrator on a subscription

18
00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.008
are privileged, and Microsoft
Learn's Azure R B A C

19
00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:18.740
best practices say at most
three subscription owners.

20
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.240
Four guards keep people
from losing access.

21
00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.307
Eligible first: each standing assignment
held by a user or group gets an eligible

22
00:01:26.307 --> 00:01:28.349
replacement before its removal,

23
00:01:28.349 --> 00:01:31.752
so no holder loses the role
before they can activate it.

24
00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:37.104
Rule A Z oh five flags a standing assignment
held by the identity the plan runs as;

25
00:01:37.104 --> 00:01:40.104
the apply needs it to
finish, so decide it last,

26
00:01:40.104 --> 00:01:44.240
from another session, as this
workbook's design guidance says.

27
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.272
The desired state refuses an
authentication context before its

28
00:01:48.272 --> 00:01:52.527
Conditional Access policy is
confirmed, as Microsoft Learn advises.

29
00:01:52.527 --> 00:01:57.700
And policies go first, because a
request that breaks a policy is refused.

30
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.261
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

31
00:02:04.261 --> 00:02:06.625
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

32
00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:11.209
You'll explain why PIM for Azure
resources goes through Resource Manager,

33
00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:13.388
and which A P I version the code uses.

34
00:02:13.388 --> 00:02:17.563
You'll explain why role settings
must be set at every governed scope.

35
00:02:17.563 --> 00:02:20.351
You'll say who can and
can't be made eligible.

36
00:02:20.351 --> 00:02:23.689
You'll read the plan's
three kinds of operation

37
00:02:23.689 --> 00:02:26.150
and its findings, A Z oh one to oh six.

38
00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:30.350
And you'll explain why the code
declares User Access Administrator,

39
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.775
not Owner, for the apply.

40
00:02:31.775 --> 00:02:36.346
Before you start, finish Module 1,
whose Azure half reads the same two

41
00:02:36.346 --> 00:02:41.162
schedule lists, and Module 4, whose
seven managed rules are the same here.

42
00:02:41.162 --> 00:02:45.921
Lab 2 needs a worldwide or G C C test
tenant with a test management group,

43
00:02:45.921 --> 00:02:49.449
subscription and resource
group, never production,

44
00:02:49.449 --> 00:02:53.180
and P2 or Governance
licenses for eligible users.

45
00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.392
Unit 1: Resource Manager,
scopes and holders.

46
00:03:00.392 --> 00:03:02.332
Resource Manager, not Graph:

47
00:03:02.332 --> 00:03:07.138
three resource types under Microsoft
dot Authorization do the work,

48
00:03:07.138 --> 00:03:12.534
and Microsoft Learn's page on the Azure R
B A C A P I versions marks twenty twenty,

49
00:03:12.534 --> 00:03:17.593
ten, oh one as the generally available
release of every PIM resource type.

50
00:03:17.593 --> 00:03:18.755
The code pins it.

51
00:03:18.755 --> 00:03:23.760
Policies don't inherit: Microsoft Learn's
page on Azure resource role settings says

52
00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.111
settings at a subscription aren't
inherited by a resource group under it,

53
00:03:28.111 --> 00:03:30.505
so the file lists every scope it governs.

54
00:03:30.505 --> 00:03:33.473
Who can be eligible:
users, not applications,

55
00:03:33.473 --> 00:03:39.243
service principals or managed identities,
which can't perform the activation steps.

56
00:03:39.243 --> 00:03:44.255
So a standing Contributor held by a deployment
pipeline is reported, and left alone.

57
00:03:44.255 --> 00:03:48.367
And what counts as privileged: Learn's
privileged administrator roles,

58
00:03:48.367 --> 00:03:51.433
of which the chapter governs
the three the plan names,

59
00:03:51.433 --> 00:03:55.760
with Microsoft's best practice of
at most three subscription owners.

60
00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:02.560
The findings, A Z oh one to oh six.

61
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.492
Oh one is standing Owner, Contributor or
User Access Administrator outside the

62
00:04:07.492 --> 00:04:12.872
exceptions, against Microsoft Learn's advice
of zero permanently active assignments.

63
00:04:12.872 --> 00:04:16.247
It's Info for a holder on the
exceptions list with an owner.

64
00:04:16.247 --> 00:04:21.160
Oh two is a standing assignment held by
a service principal or managed identity,

65
00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.211
which Microsoft Learn says
can't be made eligible.

66
00:04:24.211 --> 00:04:27.587
Oh three is User Access
Administrator at root scope,

67
00:04:27.587 --> 00:04:32.298
the elevated access Microsoft Learn
says to remove when the task is done.

68
00:04:32.298 --> 00:04:37.572
Oh four is an eligibility the file doesn't
declare, or one that never expires.

69
00:04:37.572 --> 00:04:40.322
It's reported, never removed by the plan.

70
00:04:40.322 --> 00:04:45.247
Oh five is the plan's own identity holding
a standing role, the kit's own design.

71
00:04:45.247 --> 00:04:50.280
And oh six is more than three Owners on
a subscription, counted after the plan.

72
00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.568
Unit 2: the plan and the apply.

73
00:04:56.568 --> 00:04:58.555
The call list has two tasks.

74
00:04:58.555 --> 00:05:02.080
Reader reads; User Access
Administrator applies.

75
00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.397
Microsoft Learn's PIM pages name two
roles that can manage assignments,

76
00:05:06.397 --> 00:05:09.324
and the code follows the
stricter statement and

77
00:05:09.324 --> 00:05:12.617
declares the narrower one,
User Access Administrator.

78
00:05:12.617 --> 00:05:14.405
Owner isn't needed for anything.

79
00:05:14.405 --> 00:05:17.483
The plan's order is fixed:
policy updates first,

80
00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:22.180
then new eligible assignments, then
removal of standing assignments.

81
00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:26.705
Each request has a G U I D derived
from the run I D and the operation,

82
00:05:26.705 --> 00:05:31.742
so the dry run and the apply send
exactly the requests the reviewer read.

83
00:05:31.742 --> 00:05:37.081
An eligibility the file doesn't declare
is reported, never removed by the plan.

84
00:05:37.081 --> 00:05:41.955
And every run writes a state file,
which holds each policy's rules as read.

85
00:05:41.955 --> 00:05:43.500
That's the back-out.

86
00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:47.975
Try it.

87
00:05:47.975 --> 00:05:51.388
Lab 1 runs in one PowerShell 7 session.

88
00:05:51.388 --> 00:05:56.612
The fixture estate has a management group,
contoso root, a workload subscription,

89
00:05:56.612 --> 00:06:00.588
a control-plane subscription, and
a resource group, R G payroll.

90
00:06:00.588 --> 00:06:03.571
The identity that would
run the apply is the

91
00:06:03.571 --> 00:06:06.887
fictional service principal
S P P I M automation.

92
00:06:06.887 --> 00:06:13.688
Step one replays Microsoft's examples from
the Azure R E S T A P I specifications.

93
00:06:13.688 --> 00:06:17.826
Six pass; the thirty-two not run
