94
00:06:17.826 --> 00:06:21.617
The second test is the one to
notice: Microsoft's own assignment

95
00:06:21.617 --> 00:06:25.700
example is an activation, though
its assignment type says Assigned.

96
00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:28.800
Step two lists the roles each task needs.

97
00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.088
Reading needs Reader.

98
00:06:30.088 --> 00:06:33.538
Applying needs Reader and
User Access Administrator.

99
00:06:33.538 --> 00:06:35.690
Owner isn't needed for anything.

100
00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.878
Step three plans, as a dry run.

101
00:06:41.878 --> 00:06:45.649
You give it the cloud, the
fixture estate, the desired state,

102
00:06:45.649 --> 00:06:49.342
the principal I D of the identity
that would run the apply,

103
00:06:49.342 --> 00:06:53.664
so it can flag that identity's own
roles, an output folder and a run I D.

104
00:06:53.664 --> 00:06:57.774
It reads nine assignment schedules
